  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1134 PM MDT Fri Apr 17 2026\n\nSkies have cleared over most of the area and winds are generally \n10mph or less, allowing temperatures to decrease to well below \nfreezing tonight. Temperatures Saturday morning are forecast to \ndrop into the upper teens to mid 20s across the plains, with \ntemperatures in the single digits and low teens in the mountains. \nTemperatures are expected to warm above freezing around 8AM to \n9AM across the plains, increasing into the upper 40s to upper 50s\nby the mid afternoon.\n\nThe Fire Weather potential for Saturday is a bit uncertain. Models\nhave strong low to mid level winds on the backside of the trough \nas it moves east. The strongest winds look to stay to our east, \nbut 40mph 700mb winds are forecast to clip our northeastern \ncounties in the morning to early afternoon, before they move east \nwith the trough. The surface inversion should limit mixing in the \nearly morning, but as temperatures warm we could start to see some\nof those higher wind speeds mix towards the surface before they \nmove out of the area. With minimum RHs below 15% for much of the\nplains, we could see at least a few hours of critical fire \nweather conditions across the NE plains, where the stronger winds\nare expected. A Red Flag Warning will be in effect for this area \nfrom 1PM to 7PM Saturday.\n\nTemperatures will continue to warm into the middle of next week as an \nupper level ridge builds over the area. Highs are forecast to warm \nback above normal, with temps reaching into the 70s across the \nplains on Sunday and into the 80s for Tuesday and Wednesday. With \nthe warm and dry conditions, we will have to keep and eye on fire \nweather conditions each afternoon during this time period. \n\nOn Wednesday, the upper level low which is forecast to sit off the \nwest coast of the U.S. for much of the forecast leading up to this \npoint, will start to progress eastward. The exact path of the trough \nis still uncertain, but there is potential for it to impact our area \nThursday. Right now, chances are low (<35%) for precip across the \nplains on Thursday, but we will have better chances in the \nmountains, where moisture is a bit higher. A surface front will \naccompany the upper level trough, moving through at some point \nThursday into early Friday. Strength and timing of the front along\nwith precipitation chances associated with the front will depend \non the evolution of the upper level system. This system does look \nlike the best chance for precip across the plains for the next 7 \ndays (although is still doesn't look great with only around half \nof the ensemble members bringing precip to the plains during this \ntime).",
